\title{OPT-F: the loader thread, second pass - where its page faults come from, a scanner that does not wait for its numbers, a name index that is asked less often, and what huge pages give the executable and can cost it}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{loader}
\tag{store}
\tag{performance}
\tag{platform}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Second optimisation wave after the first release, agent "OPT-F", 2026-11-25 (10:47 to 16:50 UTC). The lever is the loader thread again: one thread that reads the export, links, builds the store and packs the proof blocks. [The first pass](rep-OPT-B) took it from 12.1 to 10.6 s of CPU per Mathlib pass in the executable with loops without a call; [what it left](ins-2B4K) is memory, one chain of dependent loads and the record kinds it had no loop for. The task: itemise the thread again on the main tree as it is now, say where the page faults of the executable's loader come from and whether anything in \code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-load} and \code{rlvm-store} removes them, and take the next items in the order of their measured size, with the store word for word as it was. Nothing in the repository was changed by this work: the result is three patches, measured and gated on a private copy of the tracked sources. \code{rlvm-machine} and \code{rlvm-level} were not touched.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\table{
\tr{\th{base -> candidate, interleaved rounds on a busy host (section 1)}\th{Init}\th{cslib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{the executable: instructions of one load, G}\td{5.99 -> 5.79}\td{43.7 -> 42.2}\td{not counted}\td{108.8 -> 106.4}}
\tr{\td{the executable: user cycles of one load, G}\td{1.90 -> 1.63 (-14 \%)}\td{15.5 -> 13.2 (-15 \%)}\td{not counted}\td{37.0 -> 32.7 (-12 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the executable: the load alone, s}\td{0.512 -> 0.431 (-16 \%)}\td{4.37 -> 3.84 (-12 \%)}\td{not measured}\td{10.20 -> 9.16 (-10 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the executable: user cycles of the loader thread in a pass, G}\td{1.89 -> 1.65 (-13 \%)}\td{16.1 -> 14.1 (-12 \%)}\td{7.80 -> 6.73 (-14 \%)}\td{39.1 -> 34.9 (-11 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the executable: CPU of the loader thread in a pass, s}\td{not measured}\td{4.61 -> 3.99}\td{not measured}\td{10.81 -> 9.78}}
\tr{\td{the executable: warm pass, s (the guard)}\td{0.664 -> 0.640 (-3.6 \%)}\td{5.337 -> 5.238 (-1.9 \%)}\td{4.048 -> 3.949 (-2.4 \%)}\td{14.877 -> 14.425 (-3.0 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher: warm pass, s (the guard)}\td{0.625 -> 0.621 (-0.5 \%)}\td{4.670 -> 4.426 (-5.2 \%)}\td{3.530 -> 3.427 (-2.9 \%)}\td{12.691 -> 12.274 (-3.3 \%)}}
}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The thread, itemised again} (section 2). After the first pass the executable's loader thread costs a Mathlib pass 9.7 to 10.5 s of CPU, 119 G instructions at 2.9 per cycle. The kernel has 15 \% of it, the scanner 21 \%, and a third of it stands behind seven single load instructions: random reads in tables of 64 MB to 1.3 GB, each with a miss of the cache and a walk of the page table - the location of an earlier record, the probe of the name index, a child node, the promotion memo, a block record.}
\li{\strong{The page faults} (section 3): 867,000 per Mathlib pass in the loader thread, each recorded with the instruction that took it. Four fifths are the first touch of the store itself and of the location table, arrays that are sized from the file length and never grow; a ninth is the mapped file. None of the causes the lever named is there: not growth by doubling (under 5 \%), not arrays per unit (none), not the collector between collections. The pages are fresh in every pass because the image's collector keeps no large array for the next pass, which matters to a process that checks an export several times and to no other. What the three modules could remove is a twentieth of the faults; it was left. The copy of the whole level store at every publication is 8.7 \%, in a module this work does not touch.}
\li{\strong{What was changed} (section 4), two things. The scanner's loop takes the end of a line from loads at fixed places instead of waiting for the line's numbers, leaves numbers of nine digits to the loop of the first pass, and takes \code{lam} and \code{forallE} lines as well as \code{app} lines: nineteen lines of twenty in stretches of 43, at about 50 cycles a line where an \code{app} line took 64 and a binder line about 160. The name table keeps the names of the last thousand out of its index, which is far larger than the caches: a name whose prefix is one of them - two names of three - cannot be in the index and is not looked up there, and the index is filled a thousand names at a time with the probes overlapping. Both have the old behaviour as their definition: the stretch an interface default that makes the single calls, the name table the same id for every request.}
\li{\strong{What it gives} (section 5): the load takes 2 to 3.5 \% fewer instructions and 12 to 15 \% fewer cycles, and 10 to 16 \% less time alone on both launchers; in a Mathlib pass the loader thread needs a second less CPU (10.81 to 9.78 s). The guard holds on both launchers over three interleaved rounds: in the executable the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 2.072 to 2.022 s (-2.4 \%) and every corpus is faster, Mathlib from 14.88 to 14.43 s; on the JVM launcher the geometric mean goes down by 1.9 \%, six corpora are faster, con-leche is at +0.2 \% and reachability, whose load is 26 ms of the pass, at +2.2 \% inside the scatter of its rounds.}
\li{\strong{The store is the same store} (section 6): the differential digest of everything a load builds is identical to the base's in 25 reader configurations on 17,158 small files, the 263 arena tests below 100 MB, 847 prelude mutants and the eight corpora, under both policies; tick counts, record counts and declines of every corpus are identical with one worker.}
\li{\strong{One scanner thread stays the default} (section 5.5): with the faster loader two scanner threads are 4.5 to 16 \% slower than one on the four corpora measured.}
\li{\strong{Outside the three modules} (section 7): the executable's heap has no huge pages and the JVM launcher's has. Advising them for the image's large mappings made the warm pass of cslib and of Mathlib about 4 \% shorter in a first window; in a second one, while the host had no free block of 2 MiB, the same advice made cslib 50 \% and navier-stokes-euler 13 \% \em{slower}, and Mathlib, minutes later with free blocks again, 1 \% faster. With the host's setting an advised first touch that finds no huge page compacts memory in the faulting thread, and the image touches its large arrays for the first time in every pass. Plain advice is not a candidate for a default; the lever became a work package of its own.}
\li{\strong{Verdict} (section 11): \strong{GO} for the default configuration: a cost-only change with the store word for word the same, every gate passed with the release's counts, and the guard held on the executable and on the JVM launcher.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Base.} A private copy of the tracked sources of the main tree, taken at 10:48 UTC, built as a whole by \src{scripts/build.sh} into a private development distribution: the executable (GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 native image, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, the heap settings and the inline lists of the build) and the JVM launcher (HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 with C2, the launcher's flags, heap of the machine \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). Its jars are those of the shared distribution of 09:13 UTC, hash for hash (\code{rlvm-export} \code{\verb~|38c922c4fccd~}, \code{rlvm-load} \code{\verb~|a2a091b291fa~}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{\verb~|a93a52584ee7~}), except the machine jar, which differs in the copy of its \code{\verb~|pom.xml~} inside the jar and in no class. Its store digests equal those of the candidate of the first pass in all 25 reader configurations, file for file.}
\li{\strong{Candidate.} The same copy with the three patches of section 9, built the same way: \code{rlvm-export} \code{\verb~|8fcc3cb37172~}, \code{rlvm-load} \code{\verb~|fcf04a8669c3~}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{\verb~|825e56cff445~}; every other jar is the base's.}
\li{\strong{Symbolised images.} A second image of each with \code{\verb~|-g~} (the same code; DWARF with inlined frames) for \code{perf}, in the arena container. No guard time is from them.}
\li{\strong{Engine and run line.} A run without \code{\verb~|-X engine~}: the machine with its default switch set, policy practical, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, the reader on the loader thread: \code{\verb~|rlvm[-jvm] --threads 8 --stats -X passes=N FILE~}. "The load alone" is the same executable with \code{\verb~|-X stop-after=load~}: the reader, the loader and the store on one thread and nothing else, three loads in a process.}
\li{\strong{Instruments.} Wall clock and CPU from the pass lines; CPU, kernel time and page faults per thread from the kernel's per-thread counters, polled every 100 ms (the poller of rep-P11); shares of the JVM's loader thread from \code{\verb~|jdk.CPUTimeSample~} (2 ms of CPU per sample, attached after pass 2 of 5); shares of the image's loader thread from \code{\verb~|perf record~} in the arena container (\code{\verb~|cpu-clock~} every 0.5 ms, cycles, instructions, last-level read misses, branch mispredictions, data TLB walks, cycles stalled on memory, and \strong{every page fault} with its instruction address; attached after pass 2 of 4), every sample address resolved to its chain of inlined frames, and the hottest addresses read in the disassembly; the allocations of a load by site from \code{\verb~|jdk.ObjectAllocationOutsideTLAB~} on the JVM launcher with thread-local buffers fixed at 128 KiB, so that every array of 128 KiB or more, the size from which the image's collector gives an array a chunk of its own, is recorded with its size and stack; user cycles, user instructions and kernel cycles of the load alone from \code{\verb~|perf stat~} of whole processes, and of the \em{loader thread inside the pipelined pass} from \code{\verb~|perf stat --per-thread --no-inherit~} attached after pass 1 of 3 (counts, not times: they were not taken in quiet windows).}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB, 4 KiB pages, transparent huge pages in \code{madvise} mode. Every time in seconds was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores, heavy jobs behind \src{bench/slot.sh}. \strong{The host was not quiet}: the other agents of the wave built, counted and measured during the whole work (load average 20 to 70), and the measurement cores share memory bandwidth and the last-level cache with the other 12. Only numbers of interleaved rounds inside one window are compared.}
\li{\strong{Corpora.} Init, Std, cedar, reachability, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib, Mathlib: the exports of the benchmark tables.}
}
}

