}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 The executable: shares of the loader thread}

\p{42,808 samples of the loader thread in two profiled passes of the symbolised base image (21.4 s). By compile unit (the symbol; inlined frames belong to it):}

\table{
\tr{\th{compile unit}\th{time}\th{what the hottest address is}}
\tr{\td{kernel}\td{15.1 \%}\td{page faults (section 3)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Scanner.appStretch~}}\td{14.3 \%}\td{the instructions behind the loads of the second and the third number of a line: each waits for the length of the number before it}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Scanner.num~}, \code{ieFirst}, \code{forall}, the other templates}\td{6.4 \%}\td{\code{lam} and \code{forallE} lines: four calls of the number conversion and one of the binder info per line}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NodeCtx.writeApp~}}\td{4.9 \%}\td{the load of the header of a child node (74 \% of the unit's samples stand behind two loads)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.resolvePending~}}\td{4.8 \%}\td{the load of the location of an earlier record (86 \% behind one load)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.nameStr~} with the name table inlined}\td{4.6 \%}\td{the probe of the name index (82 \% behind one load)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.WriterImpl.records~}}\td{4.3 \%}\td{136 instructions per block record, no single address}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NameTable.internNum~}}\td{4.0 \%}\td{the probe of the name index (84 \% behind one load)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.promote~}}\td{3.9 \%}\td{the promotion memo: the header of its chunk 45 \%, the entry 18 \%, the directory 12 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|TermStore.touch~}}\td{3.7 \%}\td{the load that announces a child node}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.parse~}}\td{3.3 \%}\td{the first byte of a block record (64 \% behind one load)}}
\tr{\td{copy of the mapped file}\td{2.9 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.blockStretch~}}\td{2.5 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|IdSpace~}}\td{2.5 \%}\td{the lookup of a binder name or a constant name in the table of name ids}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.exprApps~}, \code{\verb~|Linker.apps~}}\td{3.1 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|IntListStore.intern~}}\td{2.0 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.sweep~}, \code{\verb~|TermStore.appendExport~}, \code{ref}}\td{5.2 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.seek~}, \code{pending}, \code{blockOfRecordLoader}}\td{3.0 \%}\td{the header of a chunk of the offset index (54 \% of \code{seek})}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Seven items, a third of the thread's time, are each one load.} The location of an earlier record, the probe of the name index, the header of a child node, the announcement of a child node, the promotion memo, the first byte of a block record, the offset index: in each compile unit most samples stand behind one load instruction, and that instruction also has the unit's last-level misses and its data TLB walks. \code{resolvePending} alone has 6.5 million TLB walks and 8.9 million last-level misses per pass on its one load. These are not loops that could be written better; they are random reads in tables of 64 MB to 1.3 GB through 4 KiB pages (section 7).}
\li{\strong{A table in chunks costs a miss for the chunk's header.} The promotion memo is a directory of 19,000 arrays of 4,096 entries on Mathlib. Its lookup waits longest for the \em{length} of the inner array, which the bounds check of the access reads from the array's header: another cache line, and another page, than the entry. The same holds for the offset index of the proof blocks (1,200 arrays of 64 KiB). Larger chunks were tried and not kept (section 8).}
\li{\strong{The name ids of an export are not dense for the reader.} The store interns names structurally and holds the special names before the file is read, so the first name of the file that is one of them gets an id that is not the next one, and from then on the reader translates name ids through a table (20 MB on Mathlib). Every \code{lam} and \code{forallE} line looked its binder name up there, a name the loader does not keep.}
\li{\strong{An attribution to beware of.} The debug information of the image attributes three quarters of the samples of the block writer's loop to \code{\verb~|Arrays.copyOf~}, inlined through the allocation of a chunk of the offset index, and the name index's probe to the hash of the component. The disassembly shows the loop and the load. Shares by innermost inlined frame are not reliable in this image; shares by compile unit and by address are.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The page faults of the executable's loader: where the fresh pages come from}

\p{Every page fault of the two profiled passes was recorded with the address of the instruction that took it: 1,733,971 faults of the loader thread, 867,000 per Mathlib pass. By the code that touches the page first:}

\table{
\tr{\th{who touches the page}\th{faults per pass}\th{share}\th{MiB}\th{what it is}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NodeCtx.writeApp~}, \code{writeBinder}, \code{writeConst}}\td{302,000}\td{35 \%}\td{1,180}\td{the export term region, one array sized from the length of the file: its pages as the nodes are appended}}
\tr{\td{array copies into a new array}\td{118,000}\td{14 \%}\td{460}\td{the bytes of the proof blocks into their 1 MiB chunks (92,000); the copies when a table grows (26,000)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.blockStretch~}, \code{sweep}}\td{104,500}\td{12 \%}\td{408}\td{the location table, one array sized from the length of the file}}
\tr{\td{the copy of the mapped file into the scan buffer}\td{96,000}\td{11 \%}\td{-}\td{pages of the file mapping, not of the heap: one fault per 64 KiB; the JVM takes the same}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|LevelStore~} constructor}\td{75,600}\td{8.7 \%}\td{295}\td{the copy of the whole level store that every publication of a pipelined load makes: 52 copies of 5.7 MiB}}
\tr{\td{\code{NameTable}}\td{53,600}\td{6.2 \%}\td{209}\td{rows, component bytes and the index; the index is read before it is written, so each of its pages faults twice}}
\tr{\td{the collector promoting young objects}\td{38,600}\td{4.5 \%}\td{151}\td{small arrays that live to the end of the load (the 16 KiB pages of the promotion memo, the 64 KiB chunks of the offset index) copied into the old generation}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.WriterImpl.finish~}}\td{25,000}\td{2.9 \%}\td{98}\td{block rows and the two indexes of the block records, with their doubling}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|IdSpace.growTable~}}\td{15,400}\td{1.8 \%}\td{60}\td{the table of name ids, which grows by half 21 times}}
\tr{\td{\code{DeclTable}, literals, the rest}\td{38,000}\td{4.4 \%}\td{}\td{}}
}

\p{The same from the side of the allocations: a load of cslib on the JVM launcher with 128 KiB thread-local buffers allocates 1,312 MiB on the loader thread per pass. 530 MiB is the export region and 170 MiB the location table, each allocated once; 124 MiB the 1 MiB chunks of the proof blocks; 97 MiB the three arrays of the name table; 116 MiB the 16 KiB pages of the promotion memo and 23 MiB the 64 KiB chunks of the offset index; 116 MiB the 47 copies of the level store; 40 MiB the 17 steps of the table of name ids; 28 MiB the declaration table; 33 MiB the doublings of the block indexes; the scan buffer and a line array of 3 KB per buffer are nothing.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Not growth by doubling, not arrays per unit, not the collector between collections.} The questions the lever asked have one answer: four fifths of the faults are the \em{first touch} of the store itself and of the location table. The big columns are sized from the file length and do not grow on any of the eight corpora; nothing large is allocated per unit; and the collector gives a large array back to the system when it dies, which for these arrays is the end of the pass. In a process that checks the export once, the fresh pages are simply the memory the store needs. In a process that checks it several times, the image takes them again in every pass, because [its collector keeps no large chunk for reuse](dec-EGMG), and the JVM does not, because its heap stays mapped: that is the 0.76 million faults between the two, and it exists only in the warm metric.}
\li{\strong{What can be removed inside the three modules is a twentieth.} The doublings of the block indexes and of the table of name ids, and the second fault of the name index's pages, are about 40,000 faults per pass, under 5 \%, 0.05 s. They were not changed: no measured gain stands behind them.}
\li{\strong{What can be removed outside them is larger.} The copy of the level store at every publication is 8.7 \% of the loader's faults and 295 MiB of copying per Mathlib pass, in \code{rlvm-level} and in the publication interval of the driver. And the whole table is a table of 4 KiB pages: see section 7.}
\li{\strong{Larger arrays are not cheaper.} Two changes that turn many small arrays into few large ones were measured (section 8). In this image a small array is allocated in young memory that is reused, zeroed in the cache and copied once by the collector; a large one is fresh pages. The fresh pages cost more.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The changes}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 The scanner: the end of a line before its numbers, and binder lines in the loop}

\p{An \code{app} line is three numbers between fixed texts, and the address of each number is known only when the length of the one before it is: three loads that wait for each other, and at the end of the chain the position of the next line. So the lines of a stretch were scanned one after the other, 64 cycles for 260 instructions, however wide the processor. The new loop, \code{\verb~|Scanner.exprStretch~}, takes the end of the line from three loads at fixed places instead: the first byte below \code{\verb~|0x20~} among the bytes 31 to 54 of the line, where the newline of a line of the template with numbers of up to eight digits must stand. The exact test of the template, which every byte of the line still has to pass, then only confirms that position, with a branch that is never wrong on a real file; the processor starts the next lines while the numbers of this one are still being converted.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What decides is unchanged.} The loop takes a line only if the old test of the template takes it, with the same numbers. The position from the fixed loads is a guess that the test must confirm; a window with another byte below \code{\verb~|0x20~} in front of the newline, or with none, gives a position that the test does not confirm, and the line goes to the code that was there before.}
\li{\strong{Eight digits.} A number has at most eight digits in this loop, so the byte behind the eight loaded ones is not looked at: a ninth digit stands where the template has a literal, and the test of that literal ends the stretch. That removes 19 instructions per number. Lines with a number of nine digits, the last 7 \% of Mathlib's expression records, are taken by the loop of the first pass (\code{\verb~|Scanner.appStretch~}, unchanged but for the layout of its records) and by the single-line templates.}
\li{\strong{\code{lam} and \code{forallE} lines} are 11 \% of the lines and 18 \% of the bytes of Mathlib, in stretches of 2.6 lines on average between the \code{app} lines; they went through a call per line, four calls of the number conversion and one for the binder info. They are now two more branches of the same loop, with the end of the line from five loads at fixed places, the four numbers converted in place and the binder info (\code{default}, \code{implicit}, \code{instImplicit}; 60, 24 and 16 \% of Mathlib's binders) read without a branch; \code{strictImplicit}, 0.4 \%, is left to the template of before. A stretch is now 43 lines long on average where it was 15 (counted in 150 MB from the middle of Mathlib), and nineteen lines of twenty are in one.}
\li{\strong{Stretches of three kinds} (\code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-load}). The scanner hands a stretch over as four ints per record (\code{\verb~|RecordOut.exprs~}; the default, which the tapes of the scanner threads use, makes the single calls \code{app}, \code{lam}, \code{pi}). The linker offers the longest prefix that the fast paths of the single calls would take - dense ids with a known delta, the next id, both children earlier expressions, the binder name bound - to the sink (\code{\verb~|ExportSink.exprStretch~}, default: refuse; it replaces \code{exprApps} of the first pass) and makes the single calls for a sink that refuses and for every record that is not of that kind. That a binder name is bound is one comparison: the name ids of an exporter's file come in order also when the sink's ids do not follow them, so the lookup in the table of name ids (section 2.3) is not made for a record of a stretch. The loader's loop writes the kind of the record where it wrote \code{APP} and counts the three kinds; binder name and binder info are not kept by the loader on any path.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 The name table: an index that is asked less often}

\p{Interning a name is one probe of an index of 64 MiB on Mathlib (a quarter full: 16.7 million slots for 4.27 million names), which is a miss of the cache and of the TLB every time: 0.19 microseconds per probe in the profile, 0.9 s per pass for nothing but that. The names of an export are distinct, so nearly every probe finds an empty slot. Two thirds of them are components of a name that the file introduced a few lines earlier: \code{\verb~|x._@.Mathlib.M._hygCtx._hyg.41~} is four names in a row, each with the one before it as its prefix.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A name is younger than its prefix.} The names of the last thousand are kept out of the index, in a table of 4,096 slots that stays in the first-level cache and is searched first. A name whose prefix is one of them cannot stand in the index, because every name with that prefix was interned after the prefix: it is looked up in the small table alone. A name with an older prefix is looked up in both.}
\li{\strong{The index is filled a thousand names at a time.} The slots of the names that move are loaded in a loop of their own first; those loads do not depend on each other, so the misses overlap, and the loop that fills the slots finds them in the cache.}
\li{\strong{Nothing outside the table changes.} Which names exist and which id each has is what it was: an id is the arrival of the first request for a name. The index is the writer's own; no reader on another thread sees it. \code{findStr} searches both tables.}
}
}

\p{Why the two are safe: no rule of the reader, the loader or the store changed. The stretch has the record-by-record path as its definition - an interface default that makes the single calls - and takes only the cases for which the two are the same by construction; the name table gives every request the id it gave before. The evidence is section 6. Both are cost only: the engines see the same store and count the same ticks.}
}

