\subtree{
\title{6.1 The single-run gates again, on sources with the fix of the level store}

\p{While this report was being written, a defect of the released build was found that is older than this work and outside the three modules: the instantiation caches of the level store could keep an entry across a reset ([the report of that finding](rep-INV-1)). Its fix entered the main tree at 15:49 UTC. It changes \code{LevelStore} in \code{rlvm-level} and one method of \code{NodeCtx} in \code{rlvm-store}, with their tests; the patches of this report touch none of those files, and the two sets of patches apply in either order. Everything else in this report - the gates above, the guard, every count - ran on the sources of 10:48 UTC \em{without} that fix, base and candidate alike. The gates that are single runs were then repeated on a base and a candidate that both carry it (the six files are those of the main tree byte for byte, and the fixed base's jars of the four modules \code{rlvm-level}, \code{rlvm-store}, \code{rlvm-export} and \code{rlvm-load} are those of the shared distribution built after the fix), jars only, through the JVM launcher:}

\table{
\tr{\th{gate, with the fix in base and candidate}\th{result}}
\tr{\td{the suites, on the fixed candidate's sources}\td{\code{rlvm-export} 82 tests, 0 failures, 1 skipped; \code{rlvm-store} 74, 0 failures, 1 skipped (73 and the new test of the fix); \code{rlvm-load} 81, 0 failures, 0 skipped; \code{rlvm-level} 72, 0 failures, 2 skipped (the depth limit, and the level slices, which the private copy does not have); \code{rlvm-nat}, built on the way, 54, 0 failures, 1 skipped}}
\tr{\td{\strong{store equality}: the digest of the fixed candidate in the 25 reader configurations, against the digests of the base \em{without} the fix}\td{IDENTICAL in 25 of 25 configurations (the same lists as above: 17,158 small files, 263 arena tests, 847 prelude mutants, the eight corpora; one, two and three scanner threads; both policies); the strict policy on the eight corpora and the Cedar slice is identical to the unfixed base's strict digest and to its practical digest. The fixed base with the reader defaults is identical to the unfixed base on all six lists: the fix does not change what a load builds}}
\tr{\td{the gate of the default configuration without the mutant corpora, through the jars (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus none --hostile~})}\td{PASS: 230 of 230 tests correct, none declined; the differential self-test caught; hostile suite 1,196 rows: agree 1,124, 68 tolerated accepts of kind (d), 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined - the counts of the unfixed candidate}}
\tr{\td{cost only: every corpus with one worker, fixed base against fixed candidate}\td{IDENTICAL on all eight between the fixed base and the fixed candidate, and every one of these counts is also the count of the unfixed pair through the executable (Mathlib: 574,100,326 ticks, 288,313,296 records, 120 declines of the machine, 60 units decided by the reference engine in 5,277,996 steps): none of these runs with one worker meets the defect}}
\tr{\td{the strict policy on the eight corpora, fixed candidate}\td{ACCEPT on all eight, 0 declined}}
}

\p{Not repeated with the fix: the four mutant corpora, the invariance suite, the gate through the executable, the runs with 4 workers and with scanner threads, and the guard.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. A finding outside the three modules: huge pages for the executable, a gain when the host has them free and a loss when it has not}

\p{The JVM launcher runs with \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~}, which [the measurement of its flags](rep-J1) found to be the one flag with a consistent gain. The serial collector of the native image has no such option: every page of its heap is a 4 KiB page. Sections 2.3 and 3 are what that looks like from the loader thread: seven items that are each one load with a TLB walk, and 760,000 first touches of a page per Mathlib pass; the workers take another half million faults.}

\p{\strong{The first experiment, about 11:30 UTC.} A library of thirty lines, preloaded into the \em{unchanged} base executable, that advises \code{\verb~|MADV_HUGEPAGE~} for every anonymous writable mapping of 2 MiB or more that the process makes. The image's collector commits each large array as one mapping, so the export region, the location table, the name table, the copies of the level store and the large arrays of the machine's sessions get huge pages where the host gives them on advice (this host: \code{enabled} is \code{madvise}, and so is \code{defrag}). Interleaved with the plain executable in quiet windows, warm passes with 8 workers, the median later pass of a process:}

\table{
\tr{\th{base executable, warm pass}\th{plain}\th{with the preloaded library}\th{}\th{loader thread busy, s}\th{CPU of the pass, s}\th{peak RSS, MiB}\th{rounds, plain / with the advice, ms}}
\tr{\td{cslib (6 passes, 2 rounds)}\td{4.997 s}\td{4.797 s}\td{-4.0 \%}\td{4.41 -> 4.09}\td{37.6 -> 36.8}\td{1,941 -> 1,973}\td{5158 4836 / 4885 4708}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (4 passes, 2 rounds)}\td{14.281 s}\td{13.759 s}\td{-3.7 \%}\td{12.21 -> 10.76}\td{110.5 -> 106.8}\td{4,404 -> 4,487}\td{14114 14448 / 14220 13298}}
}

\p{With the scanner loop of section 4.1 in the executable the library gave the same again on cslib in that window: 4.842 s without it and 4.663 s with it (-3.7 \%). The sign was the same in five of the six pairs of rounds.}

\p{\strong{The second experiment, 15:58 to 16:09 UTC.} The same test linked into the executable instead of preloaded, to see that the release can stay one executable: \src{scripts/build.sh} on the base's sources with two more arguments for the image builder (\code{\verb~|-H:NativeLinkerOption=-Wl,--wrap=mmap~} and, the same way, the object file of the C function below). That executable passes the build's own verification and held 1,042 of its 1,708 MiB in huge pages at the peak of a run of cslib (the plain executable none; with the preloaded library 1,234 of 1,917 MiB). Against the plain base executable, three interleaved rounds per corpus, the three corpora one after the other in one quiet window:}

\table{
\tr{\th{base executable, warm pass}\th{plain}\th{with the advice linked in}\th{}\th{loader thread busy, s}\th{CPU of the pass, s}\th{peak RSS, MiB}\th{rounds, plain / with the advice, ms}}
\tr{\td{cslib (6 passes, 3 rounds), 15:58 to 16:01}\td{6.160 s}\td{9.225 s}\td{\strong{+49.8 \%}}\td{5.90 -> 8.91}\td{41.5 -> 46.9}\td{2,055 -> 2,127}\td{6273 6030 6177 / 8395 9469 9811}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler (6 passes, 3 rounds), 16:01 to 16:04}\td{4.097 s}\td{4.619 s}\td{\strong{+12.8 \%}}\td{2.79 -> 3.69}\td{29.8 -> 31.6}\td{1,482 -> 1,498}\td{4024 4340 3926 / 5163 4733 3961}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (4 passes, 3 rounds), 16:04 to 16:09}\td{14.747 s}\td{14.598 s}\td{-1.0 \%}\td{12.56 -> 12.02}\td{113.4 -> 111.0}\td{4,410 -> 4,442}\td{14891 14754 14595 / 14503 14713 14579}}
}

\p{What the host's memory looked like inside that window, from \code{\verb~|/proc/buddyinfo~} (zone Normal) and \code{\verb~|/proc/vmstat~}; the counters are the host's, and other processes that ask for huge pages - every JVM launcher - move them too:}

\table{
\tr{\th{time, UTC}\th{what ran}\th{free blocks of 2 MiB}\th{free blocks of 4 MiB}\th{\code{compact_stall}}\th{\code{thp_fault_fallback}}}
\tr{\td{16:01:22}\td{the rounds of cslib over, navier-stokes-euler running}\td{0 (25 GB free, all of it in blocks of 1 MiB and less)}\td{0}\td{6,104,161}\td{1,529,728}}
\tr{\td{16:04:52}\td{the rounds of navier-stokes-euler over, Mathlib running}\td{1,530}\td{78}\td{6,118,556 (+14,395 in 3.5 minutes)}\td{1,534,501 (+4,773)}}
\tr{\td{16:06:53}\td{Mathlib running}\td{963}\td{12}\td{6,122,153 (+3,597 in 2 minutes)}\td{1,535,678 (+1,177)}}
\tr{\td{16:07:08 to 16:09:23, ten readings}\td{Mathlib running; the window ended at 16:09:34}\td{365 to 3,889}\td{310 to 676}\td{6,122,340 (+187 in 2.5 minutes)}\td{1,535,737 (+59)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What the advice is worth is decided by the host's memory, not by the checker.} While the host has huge pages to give, a few percent: -4.0 and -3.7 \% with the preloaded library, -1.0 \% on Mathlib with the linked function. While it has none, the same advice costs 13 to 50 \% of the pass. With \code{defrag} set to \code{madvise}, the first touch of an advised mapping that finds no free huge page makes the \em{faulting thread} compact memory before it falls back to small pages; that is the loader thread, busy 5.90 s without the advice and 8.91 s with it on cslib. The image takes its large arrays fresh in every pass (section 3), so it asks in every pass; the JVM launcher keeps its heap mapped and asks when the heap grows. In the third round of navier-stokes-euler the two executables were level again, 3961 against 3926 ms, and the reading taken after it shows free blocks again.}
\li{\strong{Plain advice is therefore not a candidate for a default}, and "about 4 \% of the warm pass", which is what the first experiment alone said, holds only with its condition. What could keep the gain without the exposure, none of it tried here: mappings that survive from pass to pass, advice that is withheld while the kernel is compacting, or collapsing the pages after the pass has touched them instead of asking at the first touch.}
\li{\strong{The lever became a work package of its own} in this wave (OPT-G: the advice built into the executable with a switch, the eight corpora, the gate), which met the same two faces in its own windows; its report is the place for the complete measurement. What stands here is the finding and the two windows above.}
\li{\strong{A timing of the JVM launcher carries the same exposure}, since it advises the same way: the guard of section 5.3 interleaves base and candidate, so both carry it, and it adds to the scatter of the rounds.}
\li{\strong{It changes what else is worth doing, under the same condition.} With huge pages a large array is cheaper than many small ones, which is the opposite of what section 8 measured without them; the items of section 2.3 that are one load with a TLB walk shrink without any change of the loader.}
}

\p{The function that was linked in, so that the experiment can be repeated (compiled with the system's C compiler; the preloaded library is the same test around the C library's own \code{mmap}):}

\pre{\verb~|
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <stddef.h>

void *__real_mmap(void *addr, size_t len, int prot, int flags, int fd, off_t off);

void *__wrap_mmap(void *addr, size_t len, int prot, int flags, int fd, off_t off) {
    void *p = __real_mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, off);
    if (p != MAP_FAILED && (flags & MAP_ANONYMOUS) && (prot & PROT_WRITE) && len >= ((size_t) 1 << 21)) {
        (void) madvise(p, len, MADV_HUGEPAGE);
    }
    return p;
}
~}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was tried and not kept, and what was not done}

\p{Each of these was built into a private executable and counted against the candidate of that hour: user cycles of the loader thread inside the pipelined pass of cslib, two interleaved rounds, and user and kernel cycles of the load alone.}

\table{
\tr{\th{variant}\th{loader thread in the cslib pass, user cycles per pass, G}\th{load of cslib alone: user / kernel cycles, G; instructions, G}\th{verdict}}
\tr{\td{base}\td{16.06 (15.96 16.16)}\td{14.02 / 1.64; 43.69}\td{}}
\tr{\td{the scanner loop of 4.1 alone}\td{not counted}\td{12.93 / 1.63; 41.88}\td{kept}}
\tr{\td{with the name index of 4.2: \strong{the candidate}}\td{14.13 (14.25 14.02); a second window 14.24 (14.36 14.12)}\td{12.25 / 1.61; 42.17}\td{kept}}
\tr{\td{candidate + the chunks of the promotion memo and of the offset index sized from the input (16 to 32 chunks instead of 19,000 and 1,200)}\td{not counted}\td{11.97 / 1.81; 42.20 against 12.13 / 1.61 for the candidate in that run}\td{not kept: 0.16 G user cycles less, 0.21 G kernel cycles more}}
\tr{\td{candidate + the references of a segment resolved a window of 512 at a time, the block records among them announced to the store, which loads what their promotion reads in loops of independent loads}\td{14.26 (14.51 14.01); Mathlib 35.82 (36.14 35.51) against 35.79 (35.89 35.69)}\td{13.31 / 1.78; 44.23 against 12.80 / 1.68 for the candidate in that run}\td{not kept: 5 \% more instructions, no fewer cycles in the pass, more alone}}
\tr{\td{the same + the shared terms a promoted record names loaded ahead + a memo for the level lists of \code{const} records}\td{14.33 (14.31 14.35)}\td{13.06 / 1.71; 44.78}\td{not kept}}
\tr{\td{candidate + that memo + the shared terms loaded ahead during a promotion, without the announcement}\td{14.07 (13.95 14.20) against 14.24 (14.36 14.12) in that window}\td{not counted}\td{not kept: 1.2 \% is inside the scatter of the rounds}}
\tr{\td{the first pass's loop for \code{app} lines with the end of the line from fixed loads and nothing else}\td{not counted}\td{13.32 / 1.64; 45.32 (cslib); Init 1.621 against 1.632 G user cycles}\td{superseded: 44 instructions more per line for 7 cycles less}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The end of a line from fixed loads is not enough alone.} It breaks the chain, and the loop then ran at 5.3 instructions per cycle instead of 4.1, but with 44 more instructions per line it gained 7 cycles of 64. The gain came when the loop also lost the instructions of the ninth digit: the chain and the instruction count both had to go.}
\li{\strong{Announcing promotions did not pay.} A promotion waits for about three misses in a row (memo, offset indexes, block bytes), 3.6 million times per Mathlib pass, and loading those places for a window of references together is the same idea as the name index. It cost a second traversal of the references (2 G instructions on cslib, 5 G on Mathlib) and gave nothing in the pass: on Mathlib, where promotion is a larger share, the loader thread took 35.82 G user cycles per pass with it and 35.79 G without (two rounds: 36.14 35.51 against 35.89 35.69).}
\li{\strong{Larger chunks for the promotion memo and the offset index} remove the miss for the chunk header (section 2.3) and the collector's copy of the small arrays, and turn reused young memory into fresh pages: the kernel took more than the cache gave back. With huge pages, when the host has them to give, this would be decided again (section 7).}
\li{\strong{Not done, with what speaks for or against each.} A location table of two bits per record (a bitmap with ranks; shared ids and block records of a unit are two interleaved runs): it would take 400 MB and 104,000 faults out of a Mathlib pass and most TLB walks out of \code{resolvePending}, and it changes the table that the scanner threads read. Sizing the block indexes and the table of name ids from the file length: 40,000 faults, 0.05 s. The copy of the level store per publication (section 3): \code{rlvm-level}. A loop for \code{const} and name lines: 5 \% of the lines, and their cost is in the store. Parsing the mapped file in place: the copy is 2.9 \% of the thread. The block writer's loop at 136 instructions per record: perhaps 30 fewer.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. The patches, and what an integrator must know}

\ul{
\li{Three unified diffs against the repository root, one per module, to be applied together: \code{rlvm-export} (\code{Scanner}, \code{RecordOut}, \code{Linker}, \code{IdSpace}, \code{ExportSink}; the tests \code{AppStretchTest}, \code{StructSink} and \code{T}; the README), \code{rlvm-store} (\code{NameTable}; the test \code{NameTableTest}; the README), \code{rlvm-load} (\code{Loader}; the README). They apply to the main tree as it was when the work ended without a failed hunk, and applied to the base they give the candidate's sources file for file. \code{rlvm-load} needs \code{rlvm-export}: it implements \code{\verb~|ExportSink.exprStretch~}. The patch of \code{rlvm-store} is independent of the other two.}
\li{\strong{An interface of the first pass is replaced, not extended}: \code{\verb~|ExportSink.exprApps~} and \code{\verb~|RecordOut.apps~} become \code{exprStretch} and \code{exprs}, with four ints per record instead of three. Both are optional with a default; the only implementations are \code{Loader} and the test sink, both in the patches. A sink outside the repository that implemented \code{exprApps} would silently get the single calls again.}
\li{No build script, no option and no default changed. Of the eight methods of \code{rlvm-store} that [the force-inline list of the machine](rep-OPT-A) names (\code{\verb~|Terms.a~}, \code{b}, \code{kind}, \code{fvMask}, \code{hasLevelParam}, \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.rd~}, \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.localRef~}, \code{\verb~|DeclTable.ctorInduct~}) none is renamed, split or touched: of \code{rlvm-store} only \code{NameTable} changes, and only its interning. No method that the patches add belongs on that list: the new loops call nothing, and the name table's small methods are inlined by the image as they are.}
\li{The scanner's loop reads up to 112 bytes behind the start of a line before it has verified that they belong to it; it leaves the lines of the last 112 bytes of a buffer to the other paths, so the padding of the buffers (16 bytes) is as it was.}
\li{The READMEs of the three modules are updated in place, with the marker of this report.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{Anything on x86-64; anything with Oracle GraalVM; a quiet host (section 1): the absolute times are those of a busy host, the differences are from interleaved rounds, and the windows of the guard waited up to 45 minutes behind those of the other agents.}
\li{The patches together with whatever the other agents of this wave change in the machine: base and candidate were both built from the sources of 10:48 UTC.}
\li{Which of the two changes gives how much in the pass: the loop of the scanner alone was counted only in the load alone (section 8).}
\li{The scanner threads' own cost with the new loop: with scanner threads the tapes replay record by record as before.}
\li{The first, cold pass of a process as a number of its own: the guard is the warm pass. The two changes do not depend on a pass having been made before.}
\li{The huge-page advice of section 7 beyond what that section has: two windows of two and three rounds on two and three corpora, one host, no gate; the preloaded library and the linked function were never timed in one window, and the free blocks of the host were not read during the first window.}
\li{With the fix of the level store (section 6.1): the mutant corpora, the invariance suite, the executable and the guard. They ran on the sources of 10:48 UTC, base and candidate alike.}
\li{More rounds of reachability on the JVM launcher: eight were queued and did not get a window.}
\li{Files that are not exporter output at speed: a file whose name ids are not in order gets no binder record in a stretch, a file with other control bytes than the newline falls back line by line; both are correct by the tests and by the mutant corpora, and neither was timed.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. Verdict}

\p{\strong{GO for the default configuration.} The reasons, in the order of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{It is a cost-only change.} The store the loader builds is the base's store word for word in every reader configuration the loader's tests know and under both policies, and the engines count the same ticks, records and declines on all eight corpora (section 6). Nothing a worker reads is different, so no verdict can be.}
\li{\strong{Every gate passed with the release's counts}, through the executable and through the jars: 230 tests, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, the hostile suite with 0 wrong accept and 0 wrong reject; the invariance suite has no schedule difference; the strict policy accepts all eight corpora with nothing declined. The two mutant runs that ran into the gate's time limit are those the first pass met; they do so under the host's load in the base as in the candidate, and both give the official verdict when run alone.}
\li{\strong{The guard holds on the executable and on the JVM launcher} (section 5.3): geometric mean -2.4 \% and -1.9 \%, the worst corpus -1.0 \% and +2.2 \%, three interleaved rounds each. Mathlib in the executable: 14.88 -> 14.43 s.}
\li{\strong{The price} is source: about 570 added lines in the three modules, 330 of them one loop of the scanner in which the conversion of a number is written out eleven times, 130 in the name table, and 260 lines of tests; and an optional interface of the first pass that changes its name and its record layout (section 9). Peak RSS is 1 to 4 \% higher on cedar in the executable and not higher on any other corpus.}
}

\p{What the verdict rests on and does not cover: the base is the main tree of 10:48 UTC, which did not yet have the fix of the level store that was published at 15:50 UTC - the store equality, the suites, the 230 tests, the hostile suite, the counts with one worker and the strict policy were repeated with it and came out the same (section 6.1), the mutant corpora, the invariance suite, the executable's gate and the guard were not; the times are differences of interleaved rounds on a host that was busy, on aarch64; the JVM launcher's +2.2 \% on reachability is inside the scatter of that corpus's rounds and was not resolved further than section 5.3 says. The integrator's own guard on the merged tree is the measurement that counts. The finding of section 7 is not part of the verdict: it is not in the patches.}
}
